## FAQs About Santa Monica Lemon Law

## Q1. Does my vehicle qualify for a CA lemon law claim?

If your vehicle was covered under warranty when the defect was first reported to the manufacturer or dealer - and they are unable to repair it - you may qualify for a lemon law claim. Be sure to check your specific warranty terms as soon as you notice something is off.

## Q2. What if my vehicle is older than 18 months or has over 18,000 miles?

You may still be eligible if the defect was reported within the warranty period - which varies from brand to brand. The 18-month / 18,000-mile window is a lemon-law presumption, not a requirement. It does not decide whether you have a good case. What matters is whether the defect first occurred during the original manufacturer warranty.

## Q3. Do I need an attorney for my lemon law case?

You are not required to hire a lemon law attorney to manage your claim - but earning rightful compensation without one will be extremely difficult. Manufacturers have high-powered legal teams to fight against lemon claims. Lemon attorneys know how to negotiate with these defense teams to fight for fair compensation.

## Q4. How long do I have to file a lemon law claim?

The statute of limitations in California is four years from the discovery of the defect. We recommend getting in touch with the manufacturer immediately after you notice a defect. Proving your vehicle is a lemon will be more difficult the longer you wait.

## Q5. What if I want a replacement vehicle instead of a buyback?

Unlike a buyback, manufacturers are not required to provide a replacement under lemon law. Both you and the manufacturer will have to agree to this.
